{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "operational_burden",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "rationale": "Categorizes each ticket by the type of operational burden from short_description, giving a genuine grouping axis directly relevant to identifying the 'most common' burdens the query asks about. Row samples (WiFi, VPN, Database, printing->Other) are sound readings of the source text."
    },
    {
      "name": "burden_reduction_percent",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.3,
      "source_groundedness": 0.15,
      "rationale": "Values are perfectly correlated one-to-one with operational_burden category (each category maps to a single fixed percent), making it largely a redundant restatement of the category. Moreover the specific percentages (32, 30, 28...) are not derivable from any information present in the table; they appear to be invented assumptions with no textual support."
    },
    {
      "name": "estimated_duration_reduction_minutes",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.2,
      "rationale": "This is the quantity the query ultimately calls for, so it is on-axis. However it is derived by multiplying an ungrounded fabricated percentage by a duration, and the table samples do not even expose closed_at/resolution duration in evidence, so the specific minute values assert a confident precision the source does not support."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.65,
  "source_groundedness": 0.4,
  "rationale": "operational_burden is a strong, well-grounded categorization that directly serves the query's need to identify common burdens. The two quantitative columns, however, rest on fabricated per-category reduction percentages with no basis in the table's content; burden_reduction_percent is also essentially a deterministic restatement of the category. As a set, the useful analytical axis is present but the numeric magnitudes central to answering 'how much would duration drop' are ungrounded inventions."
}

